To obtain the value of "x" using the MATLAB code provided, you need to substitute the values of "a," "b," and "c" from the solution set "sol" into the equation "x = a + 2b - c/3." Here's how you can do it:

```matlab
% Define symbols for variables
syms a b c p q r x

% Create system of equations with known relationships between variables
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28;
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;

% Solve system of equations for a, b, and c
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);

% Substitute values of a, b, and c from solution set sol into equation for x
x_value = subs(subs(subs(subs(x, a, sol.a), b, sol.b), c, sol.c*3), p, sol.p, 'direct');

% Display result
disp(x_value);
```

Note that this assumes that "a," "b," and "c" also appear in other equations (denoted by "p," "q," and "r") that have been solved separately. If not, you can remove the `subs()` statements involving "p," "q," and "r." The final `subs()` statement combines all substitutions required to replace every symbol with its corresponding numerical value.

Once you run this script, the value of "x" will be displayed in the command window.

