Regarding your request for finding unique words in two large texts, the implementation provided above should be efficient and scalable. The `setdiff` function computes the difference between sets, which in this case is the set of unique words in `words1` that are not in `words2`. This operation is performed using hash tables internally, making it fast and memory-efficient even for very large input sizes. So you can use this implementation without any concerns about performance or memory usage, even for extremely large texts.

One thing to note is that reading and processing such large files could potentially consume a lot of system resources, so it might be worth considering using a streamed approach instead of loading everything into memory at once. You can achieve this by using the `textscan` function instead of `fileread`, which allows you to read text one line at a time. This can greatly reduce memory usage and make the process more scalable. Here's how you could adapt the code to use streaming:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWordsStreaming(textFile1, textFile2)
    % Read lines from both text files using textscan
    [lines1, ~] = textscan(textFile1, '%s', 'Delimiter', '\n');
    [lines2, ~] = textscan(textFile2, '%s', 'Delimiter', '\n');

