To make this solution more efficient for very large text files, you may consider using a stream reader instead of loading the entire text into memory at once. Here's how you can modify the code to achieve that:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1File, text2File)
    %Read the texts line by line and process them on the fly
    text1 = textscan('%s', fopen(text1File, 'r'), 'Delimiter', '\n');
    text2 = textscan('%s', fopen(text2File, 'r'), 'Delimiter', '\n');
    close(fopen(text1File, 'r'));
    close(fopen(text2File, 'r'));

    %Convert texts to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = cellfun(@lower, text1(:), 2);
    words1 = horzcat(cellfun(@strsplit, words1, {}, 'UniformOutput', false));
    words2 = cellfun(@lower, text2(:), 2);
    words2 = horzcat(cellfun(@strsplit, words2, {}, 'UniformOutput', false));

    %Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
    uniqueWords = setdiff(unique(horzcat(words1{:}))), unique(horzcat(words2{:})));
end
```

In this modified function, `findUniqueWords()`, we use the `textscan()` function to read the text files line by line without loading them into memory all at once. We then convert each line to lowercase and split it into words using `strsplit()`. Finally, we concatenate the words arrays from both files using `horzcat()`, take the unique elements using `unique()`, and compute the difference using `setdiff()`. This approach is more memory-efficient since we're processing the text one line at a time instead of loading the entire text into memory. However, note that it may be slower than the original implementation because of the additional I/O operations involved. You can benchmark both approaches with your specific datasets to see which one suits your needs better.

