```matlab
% Set up memory mapping
map1 = mmopen('Text1.txt', 'r');
map2 = mmopen('Text2.txt', 'r');

% Read data from mapped files (similar to fscanf)
words1 = mmread(map1, '%s', [], 'Delimiter', ' ');
words2 = mmread(map2, '%s', [], 'Delimiter', ' ');

% Convert text to lowercase and split into words
words1 = lower(strsplit(words1));
words2 = lower(strsplit(words2));

% Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

% Close mapped files
mmclose(map1);
mmclose(map2);

% Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```

Memory-mapped files allow you to read and write data directly from disk without having to load it all into memory at once, which can significantly reduce memory usage and improve performance for very large files. In this case, we use `mmopen` to open the files in read mode, and then use `mmread` to read the data from the mapped files instead of using `fileread`. This allows us to process much larger files than would be possible otherwise. When we're done, we close the mapped files using `mmclose`. Note that memory-mapping is only available on platforms that support it (e.g., Linux, macOS). If you're running Windows, you won't be able to use this optimization.

