In the context of Model-View-Controller (MVC) architecture, the model and view components interact with each other through the exchange of data in the form of attributes. When new attributes are added to the model, it affects both the model and the view in different ways:

1. Updating the Model:

When a new attribute is added to the model, it needs to be initialized with some default value or set to null. This can be done by modifying the constructor or getter/setter methods of the model class. The existing code that uses this model should also be updated to handle the new attribute.

For example, let's say we have an Employee model with attributes like name, age, and designation. We decide to add a new attribute called salary. The modified Employee class would look something like this:

```java
public class Employee {
    private String name;
    private int age;
    private String designation;
    private double salary;

    public Employee() {
        // Default values for new attributes
        salary = 0.0;
    }

    // Getters and Setters
}
```

2. Updating the View:

Once the new attribute is added to the model, it needs to be displayed in the corresponding view. This can be done by adding a new field or section to the view to display the new attribute. If the attribute is required as input from the user, then a new form element should be added to collect the value.

