The `is_numeric()` function takes a vector as input and returns `TRUE` if all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric without losing any digits (i.e., no leading/trailing whitespace or decimal points). It does this by first checking if there are any missing values using `any(is.na(as.numeric(x)))`. If there are missing values, the function immediately returns `FALSE`. Otherwise, it splits the string into its constituent parts using `strsplit()`, and then checks if each part has exactly one element using `all(sapply(strsplit(x, ""), function(y) length(y) == 1))`. If any part of the string has multiple elements, the function returns `FALSE`. If all parts have exactly one element, the function converts the string to numeric using `as.numeric()` and returns `TRUE`.

