Here's an implementation of the decorator:

```typescript
import { Injectable, NestMiddleware } from '@nestjs/common';
import { ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/core';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable()
export class DevModeMiddleware implements NestMiddleware {
  use(context: ExecutionContext, next: CallHandler): Observable<any> {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const app = context.switchToApplication();
    const env = app.get('env');

    if (env === 'development') {
      return next.handle();
    } else {
      return new Observable(() => {});
    }
  }
}
```

To use this middleware, you can apply it to a controller method like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, UseGuards, Get } from '@nestjs/common';
import { AuthGuard } from './auth.guard';
import { DevModeMiddleware } from './dev-mode.middleware';

@Controller('api')
@UseGuards(AuthGuard)
export class MyController {
  @DevModeMiddleware()
  @Get()
  getData(): any {
    // ...
  }
}
```

In this example, we're applying the `DevModeMiddleware` as a middleware to the `getData()` method. If the application is running in development mode (i.e., `NODE_ENV` is set to 'development'), the middleware will allow the request to proceed to the handler function. Otherwise, it will short-circuit the request by returning an empty observable.

Note that we're injecting the middleware into the controller method using the `@DevModeMiddleware()` decorator. This allows us to easily turn on or off the middleware without having to modify the route definition itself. We're also using dependency injection to access the `ExecutionContext` and `CallHandler` objects required by the middleware.

