As for your second request, here's an example Java program that calculates the duration between two dates while considering the time zones:

```java
import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.ZoneId;
import java.time.ZonedDateTime;

public class DateDurationCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create two ZonedDateTimes in different time zones
        ZonedDateTime start = ZonedDateTime.of(2022, 1, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, ZoneId.of("Europe/London"));
        ZonedDateTime end = ZonedDateTime.of(2022, 1, 1, 15, 30, 0, 0, ZoneId.of("America/New_York"));

        // Convert both ZonedDateTimes to UTC so we can compare them directly
        ZonedDateTime utcStart = start.withZoneSameInstant(ZoneId.of("UTC"));
        ZonedDateTime utcEnd = end.withZoneSameInstant(ZoneId.of("UTC"));

        // Calculate the duration between the two UTC times
        Duration duration = Duration.between(utcStart, utcEnd);

        // Print out the result
        System.out.println("Duration: " + duration);
    }
}
```

This program first creates `ZonedDateTime` objects for the starting and ending times in their respective time zones (London and New York). It then converts both times to UTC using the `withZoneSameInstant()` method, which preserves the instant in time but changes the time zone. By comparing the resulting `ZonedDateTime` objects in UTC, we can calculate the duration between the two times as usual using the `Duration.between()` method. This ensures that the calculation takes into account any differences in time zone offsets when determining the duration.

